# The single public entrance to a Kurul deployment.
#
# Everything a browser loads — the app and the API — is served from ONE origin, and that is a
# deliberate architectural choice, not just tidiness (audit finding PM-02):
#
#   * It is what lets a published web image work on any domain. Next.js compiles
#     `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` into the JavaScript at build time, so an *absolute* API URL makes
#     the image deployment-specific and forces every self-hoster to rebuild it. A same-origin
#     path (`/api`) is already correct everywhere, so the constant can stay baked and the image
#     stays reusable. See `apps/web/lib/api-url.ts`.
#   * It removes the cross-site cookie problem: the session cookie the API sets is same-site
#     with the page that reads it, so nothing depends on `SameSite=None`.
#   * It means the API is not published on a host port at all — it is reachable only through
#     this proxy, on the container network, which is the precondition that makes
#     `TRUST_PROXY=1` on the API safe (see docker-compose.yml).
#
# Operators who already run their own reverse proxy can replace this service, but must keep the
# same three-rule contract, in this order, all under one hostname:
#
#   1. `/auth/*`  → the API, path UNCHANGED        (nginx: `proxy_pass http://api:4000;`)
#   2. `/api/*`   → the API, `/api` prefix REMOVED (nginx: `proxy_pass http://api:4000/;`)
#   3. everything else → the web app
#
# plus WebSocket upgrades passed through on rule 2, and a request body limit on both API rules:
# 64 KiB on rule 1, the API's AUTH_BODY_MAX_BYTES (nginx: `client_max_body_size 64k;`), and
# 26 MiB on rule 2, one mebibyte above the API's ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES (nginx:
# `client_max_body_size 26m;`). The body limits are part of the contract, not tuning knobs:
# Caddy imposes none by default and nginx defaults to 1 MB, so a replacement proxy that omits
# the second fails every upload over a megabyte with a 413 that appears in no documentation,
# and one that omits the first leaves the auth routes reading bodies of any size into the API's
# heap. Rules 1 and 2 differ on purpose; the comments on each below say why.
{
	# Nothing here is administered over Caddy's HTTP admin API — the config is this file, and a
	# change is a container restart. Leaving the endpoint listening (localhost:2019 inside the
	# container) would give anything that got code execution in this container a way to
	# rewrite the routing.
	admin off

	# Automatic HTTPS obtains and renews certificates for the hostname in SITE_URL below when
	# it is `https://` (the default when a bare hostname is given). ACME notification e-mail is
	# deliberately not configured: Let's Encrypt does not require one, and an `email` directive
	# with an empty value is a hard config error, which would turn "operator did not set an
	# optional variable" into "the stack does not start". Operators who want expiry warnings
	# add `email you@example.com` here.
}

# The public address. `http://localhost` (the default) serves plain HTTP on port 80 for a
# local `docker compose up` with no domain; `https://kurul.example.com` triggers automatic
# HTTPS, including the HTTP→HTTPS redirect Caddy installs on port 80 for it. The value carries
# the scheme because that is what tells Caddy which of the two to do — see
# docs/self-hosting.md.
{$SITE_URL:http://localhost} {
	# Better Auth, forwarded with its path INTACT (`handle`, not `handle_path`).
	#
	# It is the one part of the API that does not live under the `/api` prefix, and that is a
	# constraint of the library, not a style choice: Better Auth derives its mount path from the
	# `baseURL` it is configured with and matches incoming request paths against it, on the
	# server, in the browser client, and in the verification links it emails out. Those three
	# have to agree on one string. Serving it under a prefix the proxy then strips makes the
	# server expect `/api/auth/…` while receiving `/auth/…` — every sign-up answered with a 404
	# the UI reports as "could not create your account" — and makes the mailed verification link
	# point at a path that routes to the web app instead. Keeping `/auth` identical end to end
	# is what avoids all of that without giving the Nest API a deployment-dependent prefix.
	#
	# The cost is that `/auth/*` is reserved on this origin: the web app must never own a route
	# there. It does not (its sign-in pages are `/login`, `/register`, `/verify-email`).
	handle /auth/* {
		# The proxy half of the auth body ceiling, and the SAME number as the API's
		# AUTH_BODY_MAX_BYTES (apps/api/src/auth/auth-body-limit.ts). Equal on purpose, unlike the
		# /api/* pair below: an auth body is a JSON object of a few hundred bytes with no multipart
		# envelope, so both layers count the same bytes and the ordering rule ("the proxy must
		# never reject something the API would accept") holds at equality.
		#
		# It has to exist because Better Auth reads the raw request stream itself, below the
		# parsers that enforce REQUEST_BODY_MAX_BYTES on every other route (configure-app.ts), and
		# Caddy sets no limit of its own; until this block a sign-in body of any size streamed into
		# the API container's heap. The API refuses a declared Content-Length over the ceiling with
		# its own 413 envelope and cuts a chunked body at the same count by closing the connection;
		# this line is what keeps either from reaching the API at all. `two-layer-limit.spec.ts`
		# fails the build if it drops below the API constant or drifts from the nginx row in
		# docs/self-hosting.md.
		request_body {
			max_size 64KiB
		}
		reverse_proxy api:4000
	}

	# The rest of the API — `/boards`, `/workspaces/*`, `/me`, `/health/*`, `/socket.io/*`.
	# `handle_path`, which strips the matched prefix before proxying, so the API keeps serving
	# these at its own root and needs no global prefix, no per-deployment configuration, and no
	# change for the dev loop where the two apps really are on separate ports. The browser asks
	# for `/api/socket.io/…`; the API sees `/socket.io/…`, where socket.io is mounted by default.
	#
	# WebSockets need nothing extra here. Caddy's reverse_proxy detects an `Upgrade: websocket`
	# request and switches to a bidirectional stream itself; a `header_up` dance is only needed
	# on proxies that strip hop-by-hop headers by default. Verified rather than assumed: with
	# this file in place the board's realtime feed connects on the `websocket` transport
	# (HTTP 101 on `/api/socket.io/?…&transport=websocket`), not the polling fallback.
	handle_path /api/* {
		# The proxy half of the two-layer size limit, and deliberately ONE MEBIBYTE HIGHER than
		# the API's ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES (25 MiB). Do not "fix" the two into agreeing.
		#
		# The two layers do not measure the same thing. `max_size` counts the whole request body;
		# ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES counts the file part. An upload wraps the file in a multipart
		# envelope — a boundary line and a `Content-Disposition` header per part, plus the closing
		# boundary — which rides on top of the file's own bytes. Measured on the real request this
		# API receives (one `kind` field, one file part, a 26214400-byte file): 309 bytes of
		# envelope for a 5-character filename, 318 for a typical one, 563 for a 255-character one.
		#
		# So setting both layers to 25 MiB makes a file of exactly the published limit
		# unuploadable: the file clears the API's check and the envelope pushes the body past the
		# proxy's. The user sees a 413 on a file the documentation says is allowed, and the number
		# they are told to look at is the one that is not the problem — the untraceable 413 of
		# ADR 0022:170-176 in its quietest form.
		#
		# The invariant is therefore not equality but ordering: **the proxy must never reject
		# something the API would accept.** Its job is to cut absurd bodies before anything
		# buffers them; the exact file limit belongs to the API, which is the layer that can tell
		# a user which file was too big. 1 MiB of headroom is ~1860x the worst envelope measured
		# above and still ~4% of the limit. `two-layer-limit.spec.ts` fails the build if this drops
		# to or below ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES.
		#
		# Caddy sets no body limit of its own, which is why this line has to exist at all, and why
		# the published contract in docs/self-hosting.md carries an nginx `client_max_body_size`
		# row beside it (nginx defaults to 1 MB, so an operator who replaced this proxy gets the
		# broken install).
		request_body {
			max_size 26MiB
		}
		reverse_proxy api:4000
	}

	# Everything else is the Next.js app, including its `_next/*` assets and the auth proxy
	# (`apps/web/proxy.ts`). Last, and unconditional, so a path that matched neither rule can never
	# 404 here.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy web:3000
	}
}
